
# Editor
# Local store — never commit
# Node / desktop app
# Tauri generated schemas — regenerated on every build.
# Test scratch
**/*.rs.bk
*.duckdb
*.duckdb.wal
*.lance/
*.pdb
.DS_Store
.gate-runs/
.idea/
.specline-home/
.vscode/
/target
/tmp-test/
apps/desktop/dist/
apps/desktop/src-tauri/gen/
apps/desktop/src-tauri/target/
node_modules/

# Fuzzing output. The targets and their Cargo.toml are committed; the corpus,
# the artifacts and the build tree are not — a crashing input worth keeping
# becomes a test case, not a binary blob in git.
/fuzz/target
/fuzz/corpus
/fuzz/artifacts

# cargo-mutants output. A build artifact, and one that gets rewritten while the
# tool runs — which is how a mutated source file ended up committed once.
/mutants.out

# Design canvases are drafted here and published as Artifacts. The seeded
# output is a ~2MB payload with the editor baked in and is not worth a commit;
# the artboards that generate it are, so they are excepted by name.
.design/**/*.html
!.design/**/*.dc.html

# Generated from the Specline store, and deliberately not published: these are
# internal reasoning — the decision log, the journal, the open questions, the
# phase specs — rather than anything a reader of this repository needs. Run
# `specline generate specline` to write them locally. The glossary is the one
# exception, because the project's vocabulary helps somebody reading the code.
product/
.specline/
!.specline/glossary.md
